noether 0.1
## Quantum phase estimation (QPE) of U = P(2πφ) on its eigenstate |1⟩, U|1⟩ = e^{2πiφ}|1⟩.
##
## φ = 11/16 = 0.1011₂ has an exact 4-bit binary expansion, so 4 counting qubits read it out
## with certainty. std/qft.ntr follows Nielsen & Chuang (a[0] is the most significant bit), so
## counting qubit a[j] controls U^(2^(n-1-j)). After the controlled powers the counting register
## holds 2^{-n/2} Σ_y e^{2πi φ y}|y⟩ = QFT|2^n φ⟩ = QFT|11⟩, and QFT† returns |11⟩ = |1011⟩.
##
## Expected, exactly: P(a = 1011) = 1, the target stays in |1⟩ (⟨Z_t⟩ = -1), and every one of
## the 1000 shots reads 1011. For a φ without an exact n-bit expansion the peak probability
## would instead be |sin(π 2^n δ) / (2^n sin(π δ))|² with δ the distance to the nearest grid
## point.
import "std/qft.ntr"
let n = 4, phase = 11/16
def U_t: P(2πphase)_t
qubits a[n], t[1]; bits c[n]; seed 3
prepare |00001⟩
H_a
for j ∈ 0..n - 1:
    C_{a[j]}(U^(2^(n - 1 - j))_{t[0]})
QFT†_a
print |⟨10111|ψ⟩|^2 as pPeak, ⟨Z_{t[0]}⟩ as targetZ
assert |⟨10111|ψ⟩|^2 ≈ 1 ± 1e-9
assert ⟨Z_{t[0]}⟩ ≈ -1 ± 1e-9
c ← measure_a
counts ← run 1000
print counts
assert counts["1011"] == 1000
